        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Oxus civilisation</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Southern Turkmenistan (the Kopet Dag foothills and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a> in the Murghab delta), northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> (southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>), southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, southwestern Tajikistan and northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>; key sites <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonur-depe">Gonur Depe</a>, Togolok, Kelleli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altyndepe">Altyn Depe</a>, Namazga Depe, Dashly 3 and Sappali Tepe.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">It thrived c. 2500–1700 BCE; its urban Integration Era is dated c. 2400–1950 BCE by Sandro Salvatori and c. 2250–1700 BCE by Nadezhda Dubova and Bertille Lyonnet; its walled centres shrank sharply c. 1900 BCE.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">fortified towns and palaces with massive walls and gates; irrigation farming of wheat and barley; bronze, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold metalwork; “Bactrian princess” figurines; early wheeled carts and domesticated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>; a foal buried on a wagon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonur-depe">Gonur Depe</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Soviet excavations, above all by Viktor Sarianidi from 1969; later Italian, French, Russian and Iranian fieldwork; radiocarbon dating; ancient DNA (Narasimhan and colleagues, 2019); proposed loanwords in the Indo-Iranian languages.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="empire-meta">7 min read · Last reviewed October 2026</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Oxus civilisation</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zerafshan.jpg" alt="Zerafshan" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Zerafshan <span class="kid-tale-cr">Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 3,700 years ago, people lived in walled towns in Central Asia, in today’s Turkmenistan, Afghanistan and Uzbekistan.</p><p>Farmers brought water to their fields of wheat and barley through long channels.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copper.jpg" alt="Copper" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Copper <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jonathan Zander; derivative work by Materialscientist, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Craftspeople worked bronze, copper, silver and gold with great skill.</p><p>They also made pots on a turning wheel and jewellery of colourful stones.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/camel.jpg" alt="Camel" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Camel <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jjron, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Camels were first tamed in these lands, and a little clay model shows a cart pulled by a camel.</p><p>Model carts from about 5,000 years ago are the oldest sign of wheels in Central Asia.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indus-script.jpg" alt="Indus script" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Indus script <span class="kid-tale-cr">World Imaging, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Their towns traded with the Indus Valley, Iran and the lands by the Persian Gulf.</p><p>At Gonur Depe, the biggest town, archaeologists found a seal from the Indus Valley with an elephant on it.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oxus.jpg" alt="Oxus" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Oxus <span class="kid-tale-cr">Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 3,900 years ago the walled towns shrank, and herders from the grasslands to the north mixed with the people.</p><p>The people lived on, and the Tajiks of today are among their descendants.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> civilisation was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> culture of walled towns in present-day Turkmenistan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, named the BMAC by Viktor Sarianidi in 1976.</li><li>Its farmers irrigated wheat and barley, its craftspeople worked bronze, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold, and its towns traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian Plateau</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>.</li><li>Its link with the Indo-Iranians is debated; DNA shows its people descended mainly from local farmers related to those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>, later mixing with steppe herders.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A model of a cart drawn by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>, from about 2200 BCE, was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altyndepe">Altyn Depe</a>: camels were domesticated within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> civilisation.</p></aside>
    </section>

